Render-only test of the mail mode (KMail's message viewer)

A message shown by the part in mail mode may only be laid out and painted:
nothing it contains may make the part (or WebKit) look up a name or open a
connection.  Each file in messages/ is one family of tricks (DNS
prefetching, preconnect, prefetch/preload/prerender/modulepreload,
speculation rules, <a ping> with a real click, srcset/imagesrcset, CSS
url()/@import/image-set()/@font-face, meta refresh, <base>, forms, frames,
object/embed/applet/portal, remote style sheets and fonts, favicons and
manifests, scripts of every kind, media, others); 00-control.html is plain
HTML that must render.  Every message carries a red box: the driver counts
red pixels of a screenshot (layout and paint happened).

Built with -DKWPE_RENDER_ONLY_TEST=ON (nothing of it is installed):

  libkwpepart_test.so
      the part compiled with KWPE_MAIL_TESTING: only this build reads
      KWPE_MAIL_TEST_DISABLE_LAYERS (sanitizer, settings, csp, filter,
      proxy) and switches those layers off; the installed libkwpepart has
      no such switch.
  kwpe-render-only [--phases r,e,..] [--reader2 r,e,..] [--after MS] [--wait MS] [--shot FILE] MESSAGE.html
      the driver: readers (the part from KWPE_PART, a library name, with
      "mode=mail", as KMail creates it) showing MESSAGE.html as an HTML
      part in a frame of a host document, once per phase (r: render
      only, e: external references), AFTER ms apart; the message's host
      names carry the reader and the showing (*.a2.blocked.test).  Prints
      "a PHASE n r|e", "a WIDGET x y", "a COMPLETED", "a LINK <url>" (a
      click handed to the host), "RENDERED <red pixels>" (reader a).
  kwpe-sanitize [html|css] [content|host] [render-only|external]
      the first layer alone: what WebKit gets of a document (stdin).
      KWPE_SANITIZE_STATS set: what the parse allocated and held, and
      the peak RSS, on stderr; KWPE_SANITIZE_CPU_LIMIT_MS: the parse's
      CPU time limit instead of its own.

  run.sh BUILD_DIR [OUT_DIR]
      podman containers (JOBS, default 3), each with its own network
      namespace: inner.sh gives it 10.77.0.1 with a logging DNS server
      (dnslog.py, the only resolver; it answers every name with
      10.77.0.1) and a logging HTTP server (httplog.py), a default route
      (every connection attempt sends a packet), nftables counters of DNS
      queries, TCP SYNs and other UDP packets, tcpdump on every interface.
      First sanitize-timing.py: hostile documents (deep nesting, closing
      tags the parser ignores, tags in quoted attributes and comments,
      unclosed raw text, formatting-element reconstruction, ...) and
      large ordinary ones (KMail's HTML of long plain text messages, up to
      200000 lines) must each be sanitized within LIMIT_MS (default 100)
      plus four times the time per byte of an ordinary message there,
      parsed or shown as source text: the hostile ones whose parse would
      not be linear as source text, the ordinary ones parsed; each within
      a peak RSS and an output size bound (floods of tags, clones of long
      attributes); a shape the estimate misses abandoned by the CPU time
      limit.  Then every
      message (messages/ and two generated ones: 100000 nested <div>,
      which the sanitizer shows as source text, and 1500 nested,
      flattened, whose deepest text must render) runs in a fresh driver
      process and HOME
      in these configurations (check.py gives the verdicts):
        all            every layer: no lookup, no connection, no packet
                       at all (not even to the closed proxy)
        no-<layer>     one layer off: no lookup, no connection but to the
                       part's closed proxy on 127.0.0.1, no request
        only-sanitizer, only-filter
                       that layer alone must hold everything (as "all")
        only-settings, only-csp, only-proxy
                       reported, no verdict: partial by design (what each
                       one stops alone)
        external       both showings with external references: only
                       image, style and font requests, lookups only of
                       hosts they went to; external-allowed.html must load
                       all three kinds
        transitions    render only, external, render only in one reader:
                       nothing of the render-only showings
        two-readers    one reader render only, one external at the same
                       time: nothing of the render-only one
        no-layers      every layer off: the measurement must see lookups
                       and connections (it is not blind)
      Every message is shown at least twice: a new WebKit network process
      drops DNS prefetches until it knows the system's proxy settings
      (KMail's long-lived one does not).  It exits 0 only if every run
      passed; summary.txt names each run (FAIL lines say why), every run's
      directory keeps the driver's output, the DNS and HTTP logs and the
      pcap.  Parameters (environment): IMAGE (default
      localhost/kwpe-render-only, see Containerfile), KDE3_PREFIX,
      QT3_PREFIX (mounted read-only, default /opt/kde3, /opt/kde3-qt),
      JOBS, AFTER (ms between showings, default 2500), CONFIGS, MESSAGES
      (paths inside the container: /test/messages/..).

On t590 (through the harness, reserved: ice-harness -H t590 reserve
--reason "..." --max 90m; release afterwards), in a work directory there
with the source tree in src/ and the WebKit RPMs in ctx/rpms/:

  podman build -t localhost/kwpe-render-only -f src/tests/render-only/Containerfile ctx
  podman run --rm --network=none --security-opt label=disable -v $PWD:/work -v /opt/kde3:/opt/kde3:ro \
      -v /opt/kde3-qt:/opt/kde3-qt:ro localhost/kwpe-render-only sh -c \
      'cmake -G Ninja -S /work/src -B /work/build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/kde3 \
       -DKWPE_RENDER_ONLY_TEST=ON && ninja -C /work/build'
  src/tests/render-only/run.sh build out; echo $?

Nothing is installed: the part is the build directory's libkwpepart_test.  The
container needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW in its own (rootless)
network namespace for nftables and tcpdump; nothing reaches the host's
network (--network=none).
